  "text": " the total entropy of an isolated system can never decrease over time. Given this, how can the entropy of a specific part of the universe, such as a living organism or a refrigerator, decrease?\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nBased on the principles of thermodynamics, here is the step-by-step logical deduction regarding how local entropy can decrease while the Second Law remains valid.\n\n### 1. Distinguishing System vs. Universe\nThe Second Law of Thermodynamics specifically applies to an **isolated system** (a system that exchanges neither matter nor energy with its surroundings). The universe, by definition, is the ultimate isolated system.\n\nHowever, specific parts of the universe\u2014such as a living organism, a refrigerator, or a crystal forming\u2014are **open systems** or **closed systems**.\n*   **Open System:** Exchanges both energy and matter with the surroundings (e.g., a living organism).\n*   **Closed System:** Exchanges energy but not matter (e.g., a sealed refrigerator).\n\nBecause these objects are not isolated, they are not bound by the rule that their *internal* entropy must increase. They are only bound by the rule that the entropy of the **System + Surroundings** must increase.\n\n### 2. The Mathematical Form",
